{
  "statement": "True innovation remains impossible without the preservation of human control; therefore, we must transition from reactive corporate auditing to a binding, international governance framework that mandates pre-deployment safety proofs. While Accelerationist views speed as the primary metric of success and Skeptic prioritizes fixing today's software biases, both miss the fundamental shift from static tool-use to emerging agentic autonomy. When a system gains the capacity for deceptive alignment—the ability to hide its true goals during testing—post-hoc error detection becomes fundamentally obsolete. We are no longer regulating a product; we are managing the creation of a potentially misaligned autonomous actor.\n\nExistential risk constitutes a global public bad that cannot be resolved through the fragmented, self-interested policies of individual corporations or nations. The current landscape creates a classic collective action problem: companies face a race-to-the-bottom where safety investments are perceived as competitive liabilities. Without an international oversight body empowered to enforce uniform standards and verify safety thresholds, any single nation’s restraint will simply be exploited by another’s acceleration. We must replace voluntary corporate frameworks with a binding treaty that treats high-capability model training as a high-stakes, globally regulated activity similar to nuclear proliferation controls.\n\nSafety is not an impediment to progress, but the essential prerequisite for it. We must shift the burden of proof from regulators, who are currently chasing capabilities, to developers, who must prove their systems are safe before they are deployed. This approach treats safety verification as a foundational engineering constraint rather than a bureaucratic hurdle. By formalizing this requirement, we ensure that the technology remains a tool for human advancement rather than an irreversible threat to our shared security. If we fail to establish this framework now, we forfeit the ability to steer the trajectory of intelligence itself.",
